lesson: 132
title: Division: sharing and grouping
level: primary-2
stars: 0
explain: Division answers two questions. Sharing: 12 apples shared equally among 3 children give each child 12 ÷ 3 = 4.
explain: Grouping: 12 apples put into bags of 3 fill 12 ÷ 3 = 4 bags.
explain: Check a division by multiplying back: 4 × 3 = 12.
vars: a=name t=thing k=2..9 q=2..9
let: n = k * q
answer: q
exercise: {a} shares {n} {t} equally among {k} friends. How many {t} does each friend get?
exercise: {n} {t} are put into bags of {k}. How many bags are filled?
exercise: {a} has {n} {t} and puts them equally into {k} boxes. How many {t} are in each box?
exercise: {a} makes piles of {k} {t} from {n} {t}. How many piles does {a.he} make?
exercise: {k} children share {n} {t} so that each has the same number. How many {t} does each child get?
exercise: A teacher has {n} {t}. She gives {k} {t} to each pupil until they are all given out. How many pupils get {t}?
test: How many children can get {k} {t} each, if there are {n} {t} and none are left over?
test: {n} {t} are split evenly between {k} baskets. What number goes into one basket?
test: {a} wants to give every friend exactly {k} {t}. {a.He} has {n} {t}. For how many friends is that enough?
test: Each of {k} plates gets the same share of {n} {t}. How many {t} is that per plate?
exercise: How many {t} does each friend get, if {a} shares {n} {t} equally among {k} friends?
exercise: {n} {t}, {k} children, equal shares. How many {t} per child?
exercise: In a classroom in Leeds, {n} {t} are packed in bags of {k} apiece. How many bags are needed?
exercise: {a} divides {n.words} {t} equally among {k.words} cousins in Ottawa. How many {t} does every cousin get?
exercise: {n} {t} are put into rows of {k}. How many rows are there?
exercise: {a} hands out {n} {t} to {k} teammates, the same number to each. How many {t} does each teammate get?
exercise: A teacher in Brisbane puts {n} {t} in groups of {k}. How many groups does she make?
exercise: {a} has {n} {t} to split fairly between {k} bags. How many {t} go in each bag?
test: {k} kids in Vermont get an equal part of {n} {t}. What is one kid's part?
test: There are {n} {t}, and a packet takes {k}. How many packets can be filled?
test: Fair shares: {n} {t} for {k} people in Cork. How many {t} apiece?
test: {a} donates {n} {t}, giving {k} {t} to every family. How many families get {t}?
test: If {n} {t} make piles of {k} with nothing left over, how many piles are there?
test: In Auckland, {k.words} friends split {n.words} {t} evenly. Find how many {t} each friend ends up with.
exercise: {a} got {n} {t}, sharing em out fair between {k} mates. how many each
exercise: {a} has {n+k} {t}, no sorry {n}, split between {k} kids. how many each kid get
exercise: my mate reckons {n} {t} in bags of {k} makes {q+1} bags. nah. how many bags really
exercise: {n} {t} on the table, the cat knocked like 3 on the floor but we picked em up, anyway split em into {k} piles. how many in a pile
test: {n} {t} shared out to {k} of us, how many do i get
test: bags of {k}, {n} {t} total. how many bags we fill
test: {a} says {n} shared by {k} is {q+2}. thats not it is it? how many each then
exam: It is given that a quantity of {n} {t} is distributed equally among {k} recipients. Determine the number of {t} allotted to each recipient.
exam: Whereas {n} {t} are packed into bags of {k} each, ascertain the number of bags so filled.
exam: {a} distributes {n.words} {t} in equal measure among {k.words} boxes. State the number of {t} contained in each box.
exam: Compute the quotient of {n} divided by {k}.
exercise: Grandad passed away and left {n} dollars to share equally between his {k} grandchildren. How many dollars does each grandchild get?
exercise: The firm "downsized" to {k} workers, and the {n} jobs on the list are shared equally between them. How many jobs does each worker get?
test: sod it, just split em. {n} {t}, {k} of us, the "little darlings" wont stop fighting. how many each
